# Kilosort4 that actually runs here: right CUDA arch, right numpy, right API.
#
# Three separate incompatibilities had to be unpicked, and each one only became
# visible after the previous was fixed.
#
# 1. **CUDA architecture.** The stock `spikeinterface/kilosort4-base` ships
#    torch 2.7.1+cu118, whose compiled architectures stop at sm_90 (Hopper).
#    This box has an RTX 5060 Laptop at sm_120 (Blackwell), with no
#    compute_120 PTX entry for a JIT fallback, so every run died with
#    `CUDA error: no kernel image is available for execution on the device`.
#    Not a driver or container-toolkit fault -- the container enumerates the
#    GPU correctly. The same torch version in its cu128 build carries sm_120.
#
# 2. **numpy.** `pip install --force-reinstall torch` reinstalls the whole
#    dependency tree and pulled numpy 2.5, which the image's numba refuses:
#    `Numba needs NumPy 2.4 or less. Got NumPy 2.5.` That was self-inflicted by
#    the fix for (1) and is why numpy is pinned back below.
#
# 3. **SpikeInterface <-> Kilosort API.** SI 0.102.3's wrapper unpacks exactly
#    16 values from `get_run_parameters(ops)` and its newest version guard is
#    `4.0.34`. The image ships kilosort 4.1.7, which returns a different
#    number, so the run reached the sorter and died on
#    `ValueError: too many values to unpack (expected 16)`. SI guards only the
#    *lower* bound (>= 4.0.16), so a too-new kilosort passes the check and
#    fails later. Pinned to the newest release SI 0.102.3 explicitly handles.
FROM spikeinterface/kilosort4-base:latest

# Pin the version, not just the index: an unpinned install against the cu128
# index resolved to torch 2.11.0, four minors past what the image was built
# against, and broke the torchvision/torchaudio pins. Only the CUDA flavour
# should change.
RUN pip install --force-reinstall --no-cache-dir \
        --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128 \
        torch==2.7.1 torchvision==0.22.1 torchaudio==2.7.1

# Repair what the force-reinstall dragged in, and align kilosort with the
# SpikeInterface that will drive it.
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir "numpy<2.5" "kilosort==4.0.34"

# What can be checked without a GPU is which wheels landed. The architecture
# check needs `--gpus` and therefore cannot run in a build; it lives in
# `docker/verify_ks4_cu128.sh`.
RUN python -c "import torch, numpy, kilosort; \
    assert torch.__version__.startswith('2.7.1+cu128'), torch.__version__; \
    assert numpy.__version__ < '2.5', numpy.__version__; \
    assert kilosort.__version__ == '4.0.34', kilosort.__version__; \
    import numba; \
    print('wheels ok:', torch.__version__, 'numpy', numpy.__version__, \
          'kilosort', kilosort.__version__)"
